Day#15 - Occlusion & Depth Perception in XR

1)What is Occlusion>
--the visual effect where real-world physical objects block or hide virtual objects. Without it, digital objects always float on top of the real world.

How Occlusion Works--
--Depth Sensing: Devices use sensors like LiDAR, stereo cameras, or depth APIs to map how far physical surfaces are from the viewer.
--Per-Pixel Rendering: The system checks if a real object is closer than a digital one and hides the parts of the digital object that sit behind the real one.
--Realism: If you put a virtual cat behind a real chair, the chair's legs will properly block parts of the cat

Types of Occlusion--
1)Environment Occlusion--
--Environment occlusion happens when virtual content interacts with physical structures like walls, furniture, or outdoor objects.
--Static Occlusion: Uses pre-scanned, unmoving 3D models of the room to hide virtual objects behind fixed structures like walls or heavy tables.
--Dynamic Occlusion: Uses real-time depth sensors (like LiDAR) to block virtual objects behind moving physical elements, such as a person walking past.
--Hard Occlusion: Sharp, binary cutting where a virtual object is either 100% visible or 100% hidden by a physical edge.
--Soft Occlusion: Blends the edges of virtual and physical objects using alpha-feathering to simulate realistic lighting and camera focus.

2)Hand/Body Occlusion--
--Hand occlusion occurs when a user's own body parts correctly block or get blocked by virtual objects, which is essential for natural interaction.
--Model-Based Hand Occlusion: Tracks hand joints to overlay an invisible 3D hand mesh that masks out any virtual content behind it.
--Depth-Based Hand Occlusion: Extracts the raw depth map of the user's hands directly from cameras to block virtual objects without needing a joint model.
--Self-Occlusion (Virtual): Occurs when one part of a virtual object blocks another part of the same virtual object from the user's view.
--Mutual Occlusion: Two-way blocking where a virtual object can hide behind a physical hand, and a physical hand can also be hidden inside a virtual object.

2)What is Depth Perception in XR?
--Perception in Extended Reality (XR) is the technology and process by which a device tracks user movements and understands the physical environment, 
primarily covering head tracking, hand tracking, and eye tracking. 
--It acts as the bridge that allows digital elements to blend naturally with the real world.

Main Types of perception--
--User Tracking: Sensors and cameras follow your head, eyes, face, and hands so the system knows where you look and how you move.
-- Algorithms map room depth, reconstruct 3D spaces, and label physical objects so virtual items can interact with real walls or floors.

Real World Examples--
--Apple Vision Pro
-Uses scene understanding and depth sensing so virtual objects can correctly appear behind furniture or walls.
--Meta Quest 3
-Uses environment depth APIs for mixed reality experiences where virtual content interacts naturally with the room.
--Microsoft HoloLens 2
-Uses spatial mapping and depth information to correctly render holograms around real-world objects.

